      "correspondence": "First group the convolution by t=x+y. The t=0 term vanishes by nontriviality of χφ and scaling x by t gives Gψ(χφ)J=Gψ(χ)Gψ(φ). In the Gauss product with the inverse additive character take x=uy. At y=0 the added term sums to zero by nontriviality of the multiplicative character. Additive orthogonality then leaves only u=1. Multiply the convolution identities for (χ,φ,ψ) and (χ⁻¹,φ⁻¹,ψ⁻¹) and cancel the nonzero scalar p, not a character sign. The quotient in the original R is itself a Jacobi sum.",
      "status": "mathematical proof reconstructed",
      "review": "An independent reader checked both pinned Mathlib proofs, the vanishing at t=0, the inverse additive character and the cancellation at the nonzero cardinal."
    },
    {
      "id": "JAC-02",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "title": "Why a single residual zero gives exactly one prime factor",
      "anchor": "inputs-jacobi-seven",
      "statement": "Each J_j on the cyclotomic construction route divides p in O_K. Since (p) splits as distinct degree-one primes, its order at a tested prime is one and the two Jacobi partners split that order between them. The separate finite-field residue test determines which partner vanishes. Multiplication adds orders over j.",
      "hypotheses": "Cyclotomic K=ℚ(μ_M), residue map O_K→F_p with p coprime to M, and a lifted character χ taking Mth-root values and reducing to x^((p−1)/M) on nonzero x. For terms in the product 1≤j<b<M, χ,χ^j,χ^(j+1) are nontrivial. The Eisenstein example has M=3, b=2, p=7, χ(3)=ω; the specified prime sends ω to 2.",
      "definitions": [
        "cyclotomic coefficient ring",
        "split rational prime",
        "ramification exponent",
        "prime factorization of the Jacobi product"
      ],
